---
description: Hideable snap occlusion must ignore the object being dragged
alwaysApply: true
---

# Restriction occlusion

- `hideable` snaps are dropped when a fully opaque scene hit is closer along the same ray. The invisible rectangle pick mesh does not count; its opacity is 0.
- A face-on orthographic ray hits the dragged object's own face before snap lines that sit just behind that face. Perspective rays often meet the line first, so the same model snaps in one camera and not the other.
- The occlusion test must skip the dragged node, its grouped nodes, and the nodes the transform tool is moving (`occlusionExcludedNodes`). Do not treat that object's own mesh as an occluder.
- The geometry pick radius is a distance across the hit face. A snap on that face stays valid from an angled view, even when the point is farther along the ray than the hit. A parallel surface on the hidden side of the hit plane, such as the boundary mesh 0.05 behind the lines, stays hidden.
- Grid lines and points are often the closest hit. Their raycast distance is the foot on the ray, so it sits in front of the face, and they have no face normal. Use the first hit that has a face normal. A line hit alone must not hide a snap that lies on the face behind it.
